; lang.xon -- what this bundle IS.
;
; xon: read with the ordinary reader and never evaluated, like every other
; manifest in the x-lang world (pin.xon, engine.pin.xon).  A form a reader
; does not know is a loud error, never a skip -- a manifest that silently
; ignores half of itself cannot be extended without wondering which readers
; obeyed which half.
;
; FORMAT (one form per line):
;   (lang "NAME")               the -l name
;   (dialect NAME)              he | xe | rn -- a REQUIREMENT, not a taste
;   (requires-release "TAG")    the x-lang this bundle was built against
;   (requires-lang "NAME" ["V"]) another lang this one is written on top
;                               of, optionally at an exact version
;   (entry "FILE")              loaded AFTER the dialect, not instead of it

(lang "ash")

; HELIUM PLUS WHAT ASH ACTUALLY IMPORTS, which is the whole of the boot cost.
; ash forks, execs, dup2s and opens files, and the 2024 reading of that was
; "therefore radon".  It does not follow: radon is a SET OF LIBRARIES, not a
; capability tier, and the process and file doors ash needs are one module --
; x/sys/posix, imported by name in ash/prims.x.  Everything else radon boots
; (the numeric tower: bigint, rational, complex, decimal, regex, the compile
; pipeline) is cost this bundle pays on every single invocation and never
; touches: a shell has no use for a rational.
;
; Measured on this machine, `x -l ash` start to prompt: 20.7s on rn, 2.3s on
; he.  The other six arc bundles made this same move on 2026-09-02; ash was
; last only because it was the one whose manifest argued hardest for radon.
;
; STILL A REQUIREMENT, NOT A TASTE -- the row just names a smaller one.  An
; unbound `Sys` at the first pipeline is exactly the failure the old comment
; wanted to avoid, and the import in prims.x is what prevents it: acquisition
; refuses legibly, before any shell syntax is read.
(dialect he)

; PAIRING, NOT PINNING.  A digest freezes bytes; it says nothing about whether
; those bytes still run against a platform that moved.  This row is the part
; that is checkable, and it is compared for EQUALITY, never parsed.
;
; v0.6.0 was the first release carrying what this bundle needs: bundle
; acquisition, `-l` loading, --share-dir answering from any cwd, and eval! in
; the seam.  This row tracks what the specs are green AGAINST rather than the
; floor -- the question it answers is "what was this built against", not "what
; is the oldest that would still work".
;
; v0.7.1 IS A HARD REQUIREMENT HERE, unusually for this row.  It is the first
; release pinning x-engine-c v0.1.3, and v0.1.2 does not merely fail this
; bundle -- it is dead at load.  x_prim_make_token_base assigned to the boolean
; singletons' cells rather than through them and never created the read buffer,
; so (Base make-tok) segfaulted on the first character of any input, and an
; isolated tokenizer base is the one thing a shell tokenizer cannot do without:
; 80 of 82 red for a reason that was never ash's.
;
; So on v0.7.0 this row was a promise the platform could not keep, and CI said
; so every run.  On v0.7.1 the two failures left are ash's own.

(requires-release "v0.10.0")

(entry "run.x")
